


GT307(8)	    UNIX Programmer's Manual		 GT307(8)



NNAAMMEE
     gt307 - read and set GT307 clock board

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     ggtt330077 [ --dd n ] [ --ll ] [ yyyymmmmddddhhhhmmmm..ssss ]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ggtt330077 is used to read and/or set a Computer Products Inc
     GT307 clock calendar board.  If the date argument is not
     given the board is read but not written.

     The options are:

     --dd n		 sets Daylight Savings Time (DST) on if nn
			 is 1, off if nn if 0.

     --ll                  switches output between long and short
			 formats.  The short format is suitable
			 for input to the _d_a_t_e(8) command.

     ggtt330077 is a user mode program only (not a standalone/bootable
     program like ttooyysseett ).  The kernel does not know how to read
     a GT307 clock.  Thus the recommended use of ggtt330077 is to set
     the system time from within the _r_c(8) file:

	       date `/sbin/gt307 -l`

FFIILLEESS
     None.

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     date(8), phys(2), rc(8)

BBUUGGSS
     ggtt330077 uses the pphhyyss(2) syscall to access the I/O page and
     thus can only be run by the superuser. Making this program
     setuid (so that users can read the GT307 board) could be a
     large threat to system integrity in the event of bugs in the
     program.  It is NNOOTT recommended that ggtt330077 be made setuid.

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The ggtt330077 command first appeared in 2.11BSD.   It was con-
     tributed by Robin Birch
     (robin@falstaf.demon.co.uk).

     The Makefile, manpage and integration into 2.11BSD were done
     by Steven Schultz (sms@moe.2bsd.com).








